WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:01.830
- [Narrator] SpaceX,

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.686
the Tesla Model X,

00:00:03.686 --> 00:00:05.100
xAI.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.540
- I don't know very many people

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.430
who might say they have a favorite letter

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:12.090
but with Elon Musk,
it's very clear it's X.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.100
- [Narrator] And now
Twitter joins the ranks

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.260
of the billionaire's other companies

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.870
as its name switches to X.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.640
But while the name is new,

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.430
the URL x.com isn't.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.530
It's the latest chapter in a story

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.950
that Musk started more
than two decades ago.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.960
- I think x.com could absolutely be

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.850
a multi-billion dollar bonanza.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.940
- [Narrator] Here's why the
previous x.com didn't stick

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.153
and what it's past can tell
us about the app's future.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.420
- Oh, X, X.
- Yeah?

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.153
- You will be the letter of the day?

00:00:44.153 --> 00:00:45.590
- Ah, you bet I will.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:46.980
(all cheering ang laughing)

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.550
- [Narrator] Musk launched
the original x.com in 1999

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.540
as a banking site after selling
his first startup, Zip2.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.570
- x.com, In his conception at that time,

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.660
was going to be the one-stop
shop for financial services.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.450
- [Narrator] Jimmy Soni is
the author of "The Founders,"

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.880
a book about the beginning of PayPal.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.940
He spent six years interviewing
Musk and his colleagues

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.250
about the early days of the company.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.720
- He thought it was, as he put it to me,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.300
quote, "The coolest URL on the internet."

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.890
- I thought, well, if we can combine

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.620
all types of financial services in one,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.330
so you can have, like, mortgages

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.150
and like, basically all your
entire financial relationship

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.420
seamlessly integrated
together in one place online,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.350
that would be cool.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.340
- [Narrator] According to Soni,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.160
Musk thought x.com had advantages.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.100
It was short, making it
easier to type into a browser

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.410
and helpful for naming different products.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:41.347
- X as a digit, as a
variable allows you to say,

00:01:41.347 --> 00:01:46.347
"X checking, X PayPal, X loans,
X this, X click, X that."

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.720
So the idea was that X was a kind of hub

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.300
and from it, would emerge all
these other financial spokes.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.420
- [Narrator] But this vision
for Musk's site didn't pan out.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.780
In 2000, x.com merged with Confinity,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.430
the company that owned PayPal,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.800
a peer-to-peer payment product.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.860
And founders, Musk, Max
Levchin, Peter Thiel,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.700
and other executives
disagreed on several things.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.560
One was the name.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.060
- Some of the x.com people believe

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.240
that X is a touch salacious.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.560
It's a little confusing.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.820
It's even sinister.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.980
They hear this directly from customers.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.960
- [Narrator] Money was also a problem.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.060
The company wasn't making a profit yet,

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:27.960
and was reimbursing customers
who had been defrauded online.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.120
- The PayPal folks, the Confinity team,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.377
Max and Peter's team are
saying to themselves,

00:02:32.377 --> 00:02:35.670
"Listen we don't have a lot of runway.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.410
Fraud is very, very high.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.390
The economy is clearly collapsing,

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.190
and we have this successful
product called PayPal.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.260
We should put all our
eggs in that basket."

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.630
- [Narrator] Musk was eventually
forced out of the company

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.180
and temporarily left behind x.com

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.280
and his ambitions for the site.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.830
But he came back to the URL.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:56.430
In 2017, Musk repurchased the domain,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:59.430
tweeting that "it held
sentimental value to him."

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.400
- He held onto it and held onto
the ideas for 20 plus years

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.230
and now he's trying to
bring them back to life.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.330
- [Narrator] Musk says that
the PayPal that emerged

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.160
in the years after he left the company

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.070
didn't match the vision he had for x.com.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.510
- If PayPal had executed the plan

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:13.710
that I wanted to execute on,

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.810
I think you would probably
be the most valuable company

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.073
in the world.
- Yeah.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:19.740
- Or be called X,

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.870
but it would be the most
valuable company in the world.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.240
- [Narrator] He told the
All-In podcast last year

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:25.890
prior to his purchase of Twitter

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:27.360
that he wanted to create an app

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.850
that emulates WeChat from China.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:30.683
- If you're in China,

00:03:30.683 --> 00:03:32.700
it's basically you kind of live on WeChat.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:34.320
It does everything.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.330
It's sort of like Twitter plus PayPal

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.740
plus a whole bunch of other things.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.290
And all roll into one was
actually a great interface

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.210
and it's really an excellent app.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.790
- [Narrator] Purchasing Twitter
opened up another chance,

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.160
but this time must had more to work with.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.260
- When Elon talked about
why he was buying Twitter,

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.680
he saw it as jump starting
his grander ambitions for X.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.820
And with that acquisition in late 2022,

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.470
he was getting a huge user base

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.570
that had already grown accustomed

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:06.540
to coming to Twitter on a daily basis.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:07.950
- [Narrator] Musk said in a tweet

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:11.760
that the shift to X reflects
Twitter's expanded capabilities

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.200
since he took over the site.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.240
Saying the name Twitter made more sense

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.790
when it was just 140 character messages

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.230
going back and forth.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:22.080
But he and Twitter CEO Linda Yaccarino

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.820
hinted at a future for the site

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.730
that could look more like
Musk's original vision.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:30.840
Advances in tech since
1999 could help X succeed

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.210
where x.com failed.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:37.350
- I think that when Elon was
trying to do x.com in 1999,

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.810
most people were dialing
up to get their internet.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.700
Webpages loaded super slow.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:45.390
There was no mobile app ecosystem.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:46.920
There was no iPhone.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.640
- [Narrator] But some of the
problems x.com faced in 1999

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.740
are still a problem today,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.180
like the name.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.380
- It's not clear exactly
what the rights are

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.500
for X as a company going forward,

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.420
not just in the US but around the world

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.460
with various jurisdictions.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:07.770
And it also, it's one of those things

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.200
it's not clear what the
user's gonna think of it is.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.440
Is this a dirty website
or is this going to be

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.590
the world's greatest
financial institution?

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.020
- [Narrator] And money is still an issue.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.930
The site has struggled to
win back advertising dollars

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.790
it lost during Musk's takeover

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:26.700
and it took on billions
in debt during the sale,

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:30.540
plus attempts at a superapp
haven't taken off in the US,

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.670
and regulators have voiced concerns

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.160
about social networks dabbling in finance,

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.970
like when Meta attempted a
crypto payment system in 2019.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:43.050
- The US has rules in
place to protect consumers,

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.180
and there's a lot of hurdles and steps

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.550
that involved to become a bank

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.860
or to become a financial institution.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.330
So that's one of the things

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.550
that Elon Musk will have to deal with

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:58.260
as he kind of expands what
X is or what it wants to be.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.090
- [Narrator] X didn't comment,

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.160
but people familiar with how Musk works

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.110
say not to count him out.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:09.060
- This is me curling of 45.
- Oh my gosh.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.400
- While there are lots of
really smart people out there

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:15.000
who say that being able
to replicate a superapp

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.050
here in the US

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.300
is impossible or really hard to do,

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.890
you kind of get the idea

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.870
that he's looking to prove them wrong.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:24.540
That he's going to be very
excited to show what he can do.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.667
Now if he's successful,
that's still the question.

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:30.417
(dramatic music)

